---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Risk mitigation — protecting Mom + the family from food-business liability" description="The five-layer protection plan for Mother's Gold Spice: structural (LLP), insurance, operational, legal, and financial firewalls. Plus the food-contamination playbook.">
  <main class="bg-cream min-h-screen">
    <!-- HERO -->
    <header class="border-b border-ink/10">
      <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">
        <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-spice-500 font-medium mb-4">Risk mitigation · Mom's safety net</p>
        <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl md:text-5xl text-ink mb-5 leading-tight">Protecting Mom<br/>from business liability</h1>
        <p class="text-ink/70 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed">
          Sole Proprietorship has <strong>unlimited personal liability</strong> — meaning if MGS gets sued, the court can attach mom's home, jewelry, FDs, and savings to recover damages. There is no legal wall between mom-the-person and MGS-the-business. This page covers every risk and every layer of protection — so the family wealth stays safe even in the worst-case scenarios.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5 text-sm">
          <a href="/plan" class="text-spice underline hover:text-spice-700">← Back to the launch plan</a>
          <span class="text-ink/40 mx-2">·</span>
          <a href="/setup" class="text-spice underline hover:text-spice-700">Setup checklist</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- THE RISK LANDSCAPE -->
    <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-b border-ink/10">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-2">1. The risk landscape — what mom is exposed to</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink/60 mb-6">Honest, full list. The likelihood column is realistic for pilot-scale (100–500 jars/month). Severity assumes worst plausible outcome.</p>

      <div class="rm-table-wrap">
        <table class="rm-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Risk</th><th>Likelihood</th><th>Severity</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Mild customer complaint</strong> (taste off, broken jar)</td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-high">High</span></td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-low">Low</span></td><td>Refund + replace handles it</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Customer stomach upset</strong></td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-med">Medium</span></td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-med">Medium</span></td><td>Social media risk; could go viral</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Customer hospitalization</strong> from food</td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-low">Low</span></td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-high">High</span></td><td>FSSAI complaint; license suspension possible</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Food poisoning death</strong></td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-vlow">Very low</span></td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-vhigh">Catastrophic</span></td><td>Criminal charges + civil lawsuit + media scandal</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>GST tax notice</strong> / penalty</td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-med">Medium</span></td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-low">Low</span></td><td>Late filing fines ₹100/day; max ₹10k</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>FSSAI violation</strong> (label, claims)</td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-med">Medium</span></td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-med">Medium</span></td><td>₹2 lakh fine; license cancellation possible</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Trademark dispute</strong></td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-low">Low</span></td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-med">Medium</span></td><td>Forced rebrand or licensing fee</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Kitchen fire / gas leak</strong></td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-low">Low</span></td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-high">High</span></td><td>Property damage + injury</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Helper injury</strong> (if hired)</td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-med">Medium</span></td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-med">Medium</span></td><td>Workman's comp liability</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Cyber fraud</strong> (Razorpay hack)</td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-low">Low</span></td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-med">Medium</span></td><td>Stolen funds + customer trust loss</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Family asset attachment</strong> (worst case)</td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-vlow">Very low</span></td><td><span class="rm-tag rm-tag-vhigh">Catastrophic</span></td><td>If no insurance + no LLP, mom's home can be attached to pay court-awarded damages</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="rm-callout-amber">
        <p class="rm-callout-label">The bottom line</p>
        <p>Most risks are <em>manageable with money</em> (refunds, fines, settlements). The only one that's truly catastrophic — losing mom's home or personal assets — is the one we have to <strong>structurally prevent</strong>, not just budget for. That's why the LLP upgrade (Layer 1) is the single most important move.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- LAYER 1: STRUCTURAL -->
    <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-b border-ink/10">
      <div class="rm-layer-header">
        <span class="rm-layer-num">1</span>
        <div>
          <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-1">Structural — upgrade to LLP within 6 months</h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-ink/60">The single biggest protection. Creates a legal wall between mom-the-person and MGS-the-business.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl text-ink mt-6 mb-3">What changes when you upgrade to LLP</h3>
      <div class="rm-table-wrap">
        <table class="rm-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th></th><th>Sole Proprietorship (now)</th><th>LLP (after upgrade)</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Legal entity</strong></td><td>Same as mom (no separation)</td><td>Separate legal entity from partners</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Liability</strong></td><td>Unlimited — personal assets at risk</td><td><strong>Limited to LLP's assets only</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Mom's home, FDs, jewelry</strong></td><td>Can be attached to pay business debts</td><td>✓ Protected</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Partners required</strong></td><td>1 (just mom)</td><td>Minimum 2 — e.g., mom + son, or mom + dad, or mom + trusted relative</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Setup cost</strong></td><td>~₹8k (already paid)</td><td>₹7–15k one-time</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Annual compliance</strong></td><td>~₹5–8k (CA + ITR)</td><td>~₹15–20k (Form 8 + Form 11 + audit if turnover > ₹40L + ITR)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Tax rate</strong></td><td>Individual slab (mom's slab)</td><td>Flat 30% on LLP profit</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>Year 0 — pilot validation</td><td>Year 1+ — once revenue stabilises</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl text-ink mt-8 mb-3">Why not jump to LLP from Day 1?</h3>
      <ul class="rm-bullets">
        <li><strong>Validation first.</strong> If MGS doesn't take off in 3 months, you've saved ₹15k by not jumping. Sole Prop is easier to wind down if needed.</li>
        <li><strong>LLP compliance is heavier.</strong> Form 8 + Form 11 + statutory auditor (if revenue &gt; ₹40L) + Designated Partner Identification Number (DPIN). Better to do this once you know it's worth it.</li>
        <li><strong>Insurance covers the gap.</strong> During the Sole Prop months, Product Liability Insurance (Layer 2) acts as the protection.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl text-ink mt-8 mb-3">The triggers — upgrade ASAP if any of these happen</h3>
      <ul class="rm-bullets">
        <li>Crossed <strong>₹2 lakh in revenue</strong> (proves the business is real)</li>
        <li>About to list on <strong>Amazon or any retail channel</strong> (more customer exposure → more risk)</li>
        <li>First serious customer complaint (even if resolved) — a real wake-up call</li>
        <li>Mom is going to hire any helper (workman's comp liability)</li>
        <li>Crossed 6 months of operations — enough time to know it's working</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl text-ink mt-8 mb-3">How the LLP upgrade works practically</h3>
      <ol class="rm-step-list">
        <li>Decide partners: mom (90% share, control) + son or dad (10% share, signature only). Mom remains the operator.</li>
        <li>File LLP incorporation on <a href="https://www.mca.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="rm-link">mca.gov.in</a> via Form FiLLiP (~₹2k filing fee)</li>
        <li>Get DPIN for both partners (~₹500/person)</li>
        <li>Draft LLP agreement (~₹3–5k via lawyer or LegalDocs)</li>
        <li>Apply for new PAN + GST in LLP's name</li>
        <li>Open new LLP current account; transfer Razorpay + Shiprocket to LLP details</li>
        <li>Transfer trademark + FSSAI license to LLP (mom signs assignment)</li>
        <li>Close old Sole Prop GST (or keep dormant) — typical transition takes ~3 weeks</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- LAYER 2: INSURANCE -->
    <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-b border-ink/10">
      <div class="rm-layer-header">
        <span class="rm-layer-num">2</span>
        <div>
          <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-1">Insurance — the safety net</h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-ink/60">~₹35–50k/year total. Pays for itself the first time anything serious happens.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl text-ink mt-6 mb-3">The four policies you actually need</h3>
      <div class="rm-table-wrap">
        <table class="rm-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Policy</th><th>Coverage</th><th>Annual cost</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Product Liability Insurance</strong> ⭐</td><td>Customer claims for illness, allergic reaction, contamination. Pays legal defense + court-awarded damages.</td><td>₹15,000–30,000 for ₹50L coverage</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Commercial Kitchen + Property</strong></td><td>Fire, theft, equipment damage, inventory loss at the kitchen address</td><td>₹5,000–10,000 for ₹10L coverage</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>General Liability</strong></td><td>Slip/fall on premises, third-party property damage</td><td>₹5,000–8,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Cyber Insurance</strong></td><td>Razorpay account hack, customer data breach, fraudulent transactions</td><td>₹5,000–10,000</td></tr>
            <tr class="rm-table-total"><td colspan="2"><strong>Total annual</strong></td><td><strong>₹30,000–58,000</strong></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="rm-callout">
        <p class="rm-callout-label">⭐ Get Product Liability Insurance BEFORE the first commercial sale</p>
        <p>This is non-negotiable. Insurance is harder to get + more expensive once you have a claim history. Apply during the FSSAI waiting period (Week 1) so it's active when production starts. If a customer ever files a serious complaint, this single policy is what saves mom's house.</p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl text-ink mt-8 mb-3">Recommended providers (Indian small-business food packages)</h3>
      <div class="rm-table-wrap">
        <table class="rm-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Provider</th><th>Specialty</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>HDFC ERGO — My:health Suraksha + Business</strong></td><td>Bundled product liability + property</td><td>Most popular for small food businesses; clean digital interface</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>ICICI Lombard — Business Suraksha</strong></td><td>Comprehensive small-business package</td><td>Strong for cyber + product liability combo</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Bajaj Allianz — Business Solution</strong></td><td>Modular; pay for what you need</td><td>Good for very-small-batch starts</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>New India Assurance — Shopkeeper's Policy</strong></td><td>Government insurer, traditional but reliable</td><td>Cheaper rates, slower claims</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="rm-body" style="margin-top: 16px;">
        Get quotes from at least <strong>2 providers</strong> before signing. Premiums for the same coverage can vary 30–50% between insurers. Ask specifically for "Product Liability + Property + Cyber" bundle quote with ₹50L coverage on Product Liability.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- LAYER 3: OPERATIONAL -->
    <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-b border-ink/10">
      <div class="rm-layer-header">
        <span class="rm-layer-num">3</span>
        <div>
          <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-1">Operational — prevent issues from happening</h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-ink/60">The best lawsuit is the one you never face. Disciplined manufacturing + paperwork prevents 95% of food complaints.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl text-ink mt-6 mb-3">Daily / per-batch practices</h3>
      <div class="rm-table-wrap">
        <table class="rm-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Practice</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Batch records</strong> — every batch logged with: date, ingredients sourced + supplier names, cook time, temperature, sealing time, lot number, sample retained</td><td>Required by FSSAI; critical for product recall + insurance claims. If a customer complains 6 months later, you can trace exactly what they bought.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Sample retention</strong> — keep 2 sealed jars from every batch for 18 months</td><td>Lets you test the batch if a future complaint arrives. Insurance providers often ask for this.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Per-batch QC</strong> — invert test, taste-test 1 jar, photograph the batch before shipping</td><td>Catches issues before they ship. Photos are timestamped evidence of quality at production.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>NABL lab testing</strong> every 6 months on a random batch (~₹3k)</td><td>Periodic safety validation. Strong evidence if any claim disputes quality. Required for State License later.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Pest control AMC</strong> for kitchen (~₹3–5k/year)</td><td>Required by FSSAI for State License. Reduces contamination risk significantly.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Health certificates</strong> for kitchen helpers if hired</td><td>Annual medical certificate showing no infectious diseases. Required by FSSAI; reduces contamination risk.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Photographs of every batch</strong> before shipping (10-second phone photo)</td><td>Visual evidence of quality + sealing. Defends against "jar arrived broken" disputes.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Customer complaint log</strong> — every complaint logged with: order ID, issue, resolution, cost</td><td>Spots patterns (which courier breaks most, which batch had issues). FSSAI may ask for this.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl text-ink mt-8 mb-3">The "non-negotiable" minimum SOP</h3>
      <p class="rm-body">Even if everything else slips, do these three things on every batch:</p>
      <ol class="rm-step-list">
        <li><strong>Wipe the jar rim absolutely clean before sealing.</strong> Any oil residue here ruins the induction foil seal. Use a lint-free cloth + iso-alcohol wipe.</li>
        <li><strong>Invert test 5 random jars at 40 °C for 24 hours.</strong> Zero oil staining = pass. Any seepage = batch held back.</li>
        <li><strong>Taste-test 1 jar yourself.</strong> Off taste = batch held back. No "we'll ship and see."</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- LAYER 4: LEGAL -->
    <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-b border-ink/10">
      <div class="rm-layer-header">
        <span class="rm-layer-num">4</span>
        <div>
          <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-1">Legal protections — Terms, disclaimers, lawyer-on-call</h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-ink/60">Don't keep a lawyer on retainer. But have one available + boilerplate documents ready.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl text-ink mt-6 mb-3">Three documents to draft on Day 1 (lawyer one-time ~₹10k)</h3>
      <div class="rm-table-wrap">
        <table class="rm-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>Where it lives</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Terms & Conditions</strong></td><td>Website footer + checkout</td><td>Limits liability, sets refund policy, requires resolution before litigation</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Privacy Policy</strong></td><td>Website footer</td><td>Required by Indian DPDP Act 2023; covers customer data handling</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Refund / Replacement Policy</strong></td><td>Website + WhatsApp catalog</td><td>Reduces disputes by being clear upfront. "No returns on opened food per FSSAI; replacement for damage within 48 hours."</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl text-ink mt-8 mb-3">Label-level legal protections</h3>
      <ul class="rm-bullets">
        <li><strong>Allergen warning</strong> — "Contains mustard" prominently. Required by FSSAI; reduces allergic-reaction liability.</li>
        <li><strong>Storage instructions</strong> — "Refrigerate after opening. Use a clean, dry spoon. Use within 30 days of opening."</li>
        <li><strong>Best Before date</strong> — clearly visible. Defense against "your product made me sick" claims if customer ate expired pickle.</li>
        <li><strong>Batch number</strong> — for product recall + traceability.</li>
        <li><strong>FSSAI number</strong> — legally mandatory.</li>
        <li><strong>"For consumer complaints, contact: [email]"</strong> — directs disputes to you first instead of straight to FSSAI / consumer court.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl text-ink mt-8 mb-3">When to engage a lawyer (~₹3–5k per consultation)</h3>
      <ul class="rm-bullets">
        <li>Drafting initial T&Cs + Privacy Policy + Refund Policy (one-time ~₹10k)</li>
        <li>Response to any FSSAI notice (within 7 days)</li>
        <li>Response to customer legal threat</li>
        <li>Trademark opposition / objection response</li>
        <li>Before signing any retail / distributor contract (Year 2)</li>
        <li>LLP agreement drafting (during conversion)</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- LAYER 5: FINANCIAL FIREWALLS -->
    <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-b border-ink/10">
      <div class="rm-layer-header">
        <span class="rm-layer-num">5</span>
        <div>
          <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-1">Financial firewalls — keep mom's personal wealth segregated</h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-ink/60">While still in Sole Prop, these moves reduce "what's at risk" in a worst-case attachment scenario.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <ol class="rm-step-list">
        <li><strong>Don't co-mingle funds.</strong> Mom's personal savings account ≠ business current account. Never use one to pay for the other except via documented "owner withdrawal" (treated as salary draw). Keeps personal vs business clearly separated.</li>
        <li><strong>Major personal assets in different ownership structures.</strong> Family home stays in dad's name or jointly held. Jewelry / FDs stay in mom's personal name (and may be transferred to spouse or HUF for additional protection). Spreading high-value assets reduces concentrated risk.</li>
        <li><strong>HUF (Hindu Undivided Family)</strong> can be created as a separate legal entity for family wealth. Modest setup cost (~₹5k). Assets held in HUF are more protected from any one member's business creditors. Talk to CA before setting up.</li>
        <li><strong>Term life insurance on mom</strong> (~₹50L cover for ₹3–5k/year). Not business-specific but family-protective. If anything happens to her, family is covered.</li>
        <li><strong>Health insurance with critical illness rider</strong> for mom (~₹15k/year). Food business involves long kitchen hours; health is an asset to protect.</li>
        <li><strong>Maintain 6 months personal expense buffer</strong> in mom's name in personal savings account. Should never get touched for the business. Acts as personal emergency fund.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- FOOD CONTAMINATION PLAYBOOK -->
    <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-b border-ink/10">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-2">The food contamination playbook</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink/60 mb-6">Step-by-step response if a customer reports illness from MGS pickle. Practice this scenario beforehand so you don't panic in the moment.</p>

      <ol class="rm-step-list rm-playbook">
        <li>
          <p class="rm-step-title">First message — within 4 hours</p>
          <p class="rm-step-detail">Reply compassionately using a standard script (saved as a WhatsApp Business quick-reply): <em>"We take this very seriously. To help investigate, could you share: when did you eat it? Any other symptoms? Batch number on the jar lid? Any photos of the jar or symptoms?"</em></p>
          <p class="rm-step-warning">⚠️ DO NOT admit liability in writing. No "we're so sorry, this is our fault" — just empathetic concern + investigation request.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="rm-step-title">Internal investigation — within 24 hours</p>
          <p class="rm-step-detail">Pull the retained sample jar from that batch (from your sample retention shelf). Check if any other complaints from the same batch number. Check the batch records: ingredients, cook time, sealing.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="rm-step-title">If multiple complaints from same batch</p>
          <p class="rm-step-detail">Immediately issue a <strong>batch recall</strong> via Instagram + WhatsApp + email to anyone who bought that batch. <em>"Batch [XXX] is being recalled out of caution. If you have a jar from this batch, please return for full refund + replacement."</em> This is the legally protective move — proactive recall is a defense.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="rm-step-title">If customer just wants refund</p>
          <p class="rm-step-detail">Refund full purchase + offer free replacement + apologize personally (over WhatsApp). No legal exposure beyond ₹449. Don't escalate something the customer doesn't want escalated.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="rm-step-title">If customer asks for compensation OR threatens legal action</p>
          <p class="rm-step-detail">Stop replying personally. Call the lawyer FIRST. Inform the insurance provider within 7 days (insurance terms usually require this). The lawyer drafts the response, requests medical reports, offers reasonable settlement if appropriate.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="rm-step-title">If FSSAI investigates</p>
          <p class="rm-step-detail">Cooperate fully — fighting FSSAI is a losing battle. Provide: batch records, retained sample jar, NABL lab reports, customer complaint log, photos. Worst plausible outcome: license suspension for 1–3 months. Not the end of the business; you re-apply.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="rm-step-title">After resolution — review + improve</p>
          <p class="rm-step-detail">What went wrong? Update the SOP. Was it raw ingredient contamination? Switch supplier. Was it sealing failure? Stricter foil-seal QC. Every incident is a chance to harden the operation.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- COST SUMMARY -->
    <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-b border-ink/10">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-2">What the full risk-management plan costs</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink/60 mb-6">Annual recurring cost for the full protection stack.</p>

      <div class="rm-table-wrap">
        <table class="rm-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Item</th><th>Annual cost</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>1 — Structural</td><td>LLP conversion (one-time) + annual compliance</td><td>₹15,000–20,000 (after first 6 months)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2 — Insurance</td><td>Product Liability (₹50L cover)</td><td>₹15,000–30,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2 — Insurance</td><td>Property + Cyber + General Liability bundle</td><td>₹15,000–25,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>3 — Operational</td><td>NABL periodic lab testing (twice yearly)</td><td>₹6,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>3 — Operational</td><td>Pest control AMC</td><td>₹3,000–5,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>4 — Legal</td><td>T&Cs draft (one-time) + 4 consultations/year</td><td>₹20,000 year 1; ₹10,000–15,000 thereafter</td></tr>
            <tr><td>5 — Financial</td><td>Term life insurance on mom (₹50L cover)</td><td>₹3,000–5,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>5 — Financial</td><td>Health insurance with critical illness rider</td><td>₹15,000</td></tr>
            <tr class="rm-table-total"><td colspan="2"><strong>Total annual risk-management cost</strong></td><td><strong>₹92,000–1,21,000/year</strong></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="rm-body" style="margin-top: 16px;">
        Roughly <strong>₹1 lakh/year</strong> for full peace-of-mind protection. Compare to potential downside (₹10L+ lawsuit + mom's home attachment in worst case). This is the right premium for sleep-at-night safety.
